(i) General disclosures - for each class of intangible assets, separating internally generated assets from others:
(a) whether useful lives are indefinite or finite and, if finite, the useful lives or amortisation rates used;
(b) the amortisation methods used for assets with finite lives;
(c) the gross carrying amount and accumulated amortisation (together with accumulated impairment losses) at the beginning and end of the period;
(d) the line item(s) of the statement of profit and loss in which amortisation is included;
(e) a reconciliation of the carrying amount at the beginning and end of the period showing:
- additions, separately from internal development, separate acquisition (e.g. the POS software) and business combinations;
- assets classified as held for sale under Ind AS 105, and other disposals;
- increases or decreases from revaluations and from impairment losses recognised or reversed in OCI;
- impairment losses recognised in profit or loss;
- impairment losses reversed in profit or loss;
- amortisation recognised during the period;
- net exchange differences on translation into the presentation currency and on translation of foreign operations;
- other changes in the carrying amount.
(ii) Derecognition - an intangible asset is derecognised:
(a) on disposal (for example by sale, by entering into a finance lease, or by donation); or
(b) when no future economic benefits are expected from its use or disposal.
The gain or loss (net disposal proceeds − carrying amount) is recognised in profit or loss when the asset is derecognised; it is not classified as revenue.

(I) Inconsistent accounting policies - SAE 3400
- Provision: The accountant must check that prospective financial information is prepared on a basis consistent with the historical financial statements, using appropriate accounting principles, and must consider whether:
(a) presentation is informative and not misleading;
(b) accounting policies are clearly disclosed in the notes;
(c) assumptions are adequately disclosed, stating whether they are best estimates or hypothetical, with the uncertainty and sensitivity disclosed where material;
(d) the date of preparation is disclosed;
(e) any range is not chosen in a biased or misleading way;
(f) any change in policy from the latest historical financial statements is disclosed with reasons and effect.
- Facts: Depreciation as per the Income-tax Act and finished goods at selling price are not consistent with REL's historical financial statements (and stock at selling price breaks the cost-or-NRV rule); nothing is disclosed.
- Conclusion: AKM should ask REL to correct and disclose. If presentation and disclosure remain inadequate, AKM should give a qualified or adverse opinion, or withdraw from the engagement, as appropriate.
(II) Not generating UDIN - not justified
- The view that UDIN is needed only for past figures is wrong. Under ICAI's UDIN Guidelines, a practising member must generate UDIN for all certificates, GST and tax audit reports and all other audit, assurance and attestation functions he signs. An assurance report on a project report is an assurance engagement.
(III) Consequences
- Not generating UDIN violates guidelines issued by the Council of ICAI. Under Clause (1) of Part II of the Second Schedule to the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949, a member who contravenes any guidelines issued by the Council is guilty of professional misconduct. The AKM partner who signed the report is liable.
